diff --git a/guidance.md b/guidance.md
index a6994b1..d155f85 100644
--- a/guidance.md
+++ b/guidance.md
@@ -1 +1,12 @@
 Use the provided spreadsheet tools to complete the requested workbook. Preserve source inputs.
+
+A workbook is a small model, not a guess. Read the whole sheet before editing anything: labels, units, time periods, input values and every cell comment. Comments often carry the intended business rule, so treat them as the primary specification. Then verify the layout — figure out where each requested output truly belongs from the surrounding labels, and how values flow left to right across periods and top to bottom through the statements.
+
+Rules of thumb:
+- Enter live Excel formulas rather than hard-coded numbers. Reference the cells that hold the inputs (and blank cells that will later hold previous-period outputs) so that changing an assumption recalculates the model. Build the first period, then use relative references to extend across the remaining periods.
+- Keep every original input, label, unit and intentional blank untouched. A blank the task calls intentional stays blank unless the task asks you to fill it. Do not add helper rows, headers or scratch cells to the delivered workbook.
+- Respect units, signs and scaling. Tax rates, margins and growth rates are fractions as stored; check whether a rate cell is annual or per-period.
+- When the prompt names only some years, cells, or a specific final year, fill exactly that subset.
+- After writing, reread the delivered file with recalculation and confirm that every derived cell resolves to a plausible number with no #REF!, #DIV/0!, #NAME? or #VALUE! errors, that totals reconcile with their components, and that untouched inputs are unchanged.
+- A workbook may contain more than one sheet, and the requested output may sit on a different sheet from the inputs; check every sheet before concluding a number is missing. When labels, cells or sheets are ambiguous, resolve the ambiguity from the source file rather than assuming a layout.
+- If a function returns #NAME? in this environment, that function is unsupported here; rewrite it with functions that compute instead.
